INT. BOOKMOBILE - PARKED AT LAKE OVERLOOK - DUSK

A converted school bus lined floor to ceiling with paperbacks. Fairy lights strung along the roof. A foldout desk by the driver's seat. Through the windshield: a lake the color of brass.

MAEVE O'DONNELL-KAUR (30s, librarian, cardigan with a pen in the pocket) stamps a stack of returns. Fast. Tidy.

A knock on the door glass.

SULLY (O.S.)
(muffled, outside)
You still open?

The door hisses open. SULLIVAN "SULLY" ADEBAYO (30s, rain-wet jacket, a duffel on one shoulder) steps up. He holds a paperback against his chest.

MAEVE
You're eleven years late.

SULLY
That's fair.

He sets the paperback on the desk. A dog-eared Neruda. She looks at the cover and then at him.

MAEVE
That's mine.

SULLY
It is.

Her phone buzzes on the desk. She lifts it, stares at the screen, declines the call. It buzzes again.

She silences it. Sully watches the phone go dark.

SULLY
You married.

MAEVE
Seven years in June.

SULLY
Good. That's good.

MAEVE
Don't say good like that.

SULLY
Like what?

MAEVE
Like you're being brave about it.

He laughs, then stops. The lake light turns his face gold.

SULLY
I wrote in the margins. Page forty. I never knew how to send it.

She opens the book. Page forty. A line in his handwriting, faded, underlined twice. She reads. Her mouth moves slightly, silent.

The phone buzzes a third time. She lets it.

MAEVE
It's overdue.

SULLY
How much do I owe?

MAEVE
Eleven years. Fifty cents a day.

SULLY
That's, what, two thousand dollars?

MAEVE
Two thousand and change.

SULLY
I'll pay in installments.

She closes the book against her chest, the same way he held it. The fairy lights flicker.

She picks up the phone. Holds the power button until the screen goes black. Then she sits on the edge of the desk, the book in both hands, and does not tell him to leave.
